  One way to improve your armor without using b?oEA is to try on
every suitable non-cursed piece of armor in the dungeon.  This
is bloody tedious, of course.

  Body armor should be dragon scale mail, of course.  Speed boots
are a special case, as are gauntlets of power for a Mojo'd Valk.

  If you have an amulet of reflection, it may be worth trying
every non-cursed shield in the dungeon, hoping for a +4/5.
Even a +3 shield would be 2 more than you have now.  One more 
even if it gets corroded/burnt, etc.  (Do you know both methods 
for erode-proofing armor?)  

  I do NOT recommend giving up reflection, even for 4/5 more
points of AC.

  If you're not throwing Mojo, trying every non-cursed pair of 
gloves in the dungeon might be worth it.  (If your intrinsic 
strength is high enough.  Should be possible from the Valk 
Quest).  Even if you are throwing Mojo now, a +3/4/5 pair of
gloves may be nice to have on the astral planes.  Again, if
your intrinsic strength is up to it: 18/**.

   Fort Ludious (if you can get to it) and the Castle (with the
nearby temple in the VotD) are good places to try.

  A shirt at +0 provides no extra armor protection.  It's 
probably what you should have enchanted first, as you're unlikely
to find a highly enchanted shirt.

  Not sure that a Valk has any real need for a helm of 
brilliance, enchanted or otherwise.  Enchanting your shirt
instead would have let you wear the best naturally enchanted 
headgear you can find.  (If you do decide to level up, wearing
the +5 HoB will increase your mana increases.  Not that that's
important to most Valkyries.)


  Shirt and Dragon Scale Mail are the first things to enchant up
(at least until +3).   Special items like HoB & GoD need to be
decided on a case-by-case basis.  Although I don't think GoD are
a good choice since the benefits from them don't seem to be that 
great, even highly enchanted.   Enchanting a HoB is a better idea
the more spell casting you intend to do.  So not in your case.

  You find enough shields that you can probably wear the 
second-best one until you can fooproof the best one just before
entering Gehennom, with all those firetraps.  Ditto for the rest
of your armor.

  Since you're speed boots are at +2, you probably won't find
non-magical boots worth switching them to.  
  - Boots of Lev on the Planes of Air & Water might well be worth
    it.  
  - Jumping boots, if you're intrinsically fast, will let you 
    travel over twice as fast on the planes of earth/fire, and 
    the Astral Plane.
  - Water walking boots are nice on Medusa & Juiblex, if you're 
    as clumsy as I am, but not a necessity.  (They're NOT useful
    on the Plane of Water.)

  So your boots may be the last thing you want to enchant.  
Especially since you'll also find a lot of non-cursed footgear
to test for enchantment.  "Fast" intrinsic speed is 15, and "very
fast" is, IIRC.  Not as big a difference as you might think.

  Whether +3/4/5 iron boots (or whatever) are better than +0 
speed boots is a tricky question, depending on your total AC
without boots, desire for special abilities like jumping, 
levitation, and water walking.  

  - Jumping can also come from a spell, hardly useful in your 
    case.
  - Levitation can come from a spell (not useful in your case) 
    and potions of lev, which are unreliable.
  - Water walking only comes from water walking boots.

  Boots look like the only piece of armor that might be worth
swapping around in the late game.  So leave enchanting speed
boots to last if it doesn't cost you anything.  

  Whether you start enchanting your speed boots when the rest of 
your armor is all +3 or when the rest of you armor is +4/5 is up 
to you.  Whether to wait if you don't have a shirt yet is another
question still.

  When to enchant or fooproof plain armor (+3 orcish helm, +2 
dwarfish boots, +3 leather gloves, etc.) is another good 
question.  If you're not having trouble with hit-point loss in 
battle, wait until after you finish the VotD.

  At that point, you've seen most of the armor you're going to 
see.  Also, Gehennom channels you into all those fire-traps.

  Remember, too, not to waste ?oEA on fooproofing.  There is a 
better way.


  If that seems like a lot of work, it is.  Be glad I didn't
recommend polying highly enchanted armor that you already have
a better example of.



  The summary is that you're going to find a lot of accessory 
armor (gloves, shields, helms, boots) that you can wear.  So
those are the items worth checking for enchantment as you're
more likely to find a better enchantment on one of the many
pieces you find.  Enchant your shirt and dragon scale mail first,
your boots last.



